{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":982149,"num_turns":37,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"ff613408-78b7-4e24-ad47-9e51cd635c04","total_cost_usd":3.288105,"usage":{"input_tokens":318188,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":67773,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":318631,"outputTokens":67798,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.288105,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**All 16 workloads pass with exactly zero error** (`max_abs = 0.000e+00`), at 1.02–1.06x the reference. `solution.json` and `kernel.py` are on disk and verified.\n\n## What the problem turned out to be\n\nThe headline result is negative and worth stating plainly: **this problem has almost no optimization headroom, and the reason is the tolerance.**\n\nThe tolerances (`atol` 1.675e-07, `rtol` = float32 eps, `required_matched_ratio` 0.99) were derived from *run-to-run variance of a deterministic GEMM* — which is zero. So they floor at one float32 ulp of values whose intermediate sums are ~√3072× larger than the final elements. That makes the check **effectively bitwise**. I measured what that rules out:\n\n| Approach | Elements matched (need 99%) |\n|---|---|\n| float64 recomputation of the same math | **14%** |\n| bf16 matrix-core emulation (3 / 6-term split) | 0.005% |\n| K-reduction split at 1536 … 4 | ≤11% |\n| Sequential FMA / pairwise tree / hybrid (block 1–128) | ≤13% |\n\nThe first row is the trap: **being more accurate fails.** Anything that isn't the vendor kernel's exact accumulation order loses, so the usual big lever here — bf16 matrix cores, which I measured at 822 TF/s vs fp32's 93 TF/s, an 8.8× raw throughput advantage — is unusable at any split depth.\n\nThat pins the result to a single full-K vendor fp32 GEMM. I confirmed the kernel is genuinely fixed: default, `hipblaslt`, `hipblas`, and `ck` backends all produce an **identical bit checksum** at identical speed.\n\n## The actual win\n\nThe only remaining freedom was overhead around that call. `addmm` fuses bias into the GEMM epilogue, eliminating the reference's separate read-modify-write pass over the `[batch, 18432]` fp32 output. `.chunk` returns views, so it's free. I also tested and rejected `F.linear` (equal), manual slicing, and a cached `out=` buffer (both *slower*, ~7638µs vs 7515µs total).\n\nTwo caveats on how to read this, since the scoring is proximity-to-SOL rather than speedup:\n\n- **The 1.02–1.06x is near the ceiling for this problem, but the SOL gap is large and I did not close it.** At batch ≥96 the analytic bound is compute-bound (e.g. 369µs at m=512 against ~622µs measured), so vendor fp32 GEMM runs at ~93 of ~157 TF/s peak. Closing that would require beating hipBLASLt at fp32 GEMM *bit-for-bit*, which the accumulation-order results above show is not reachable by any decomposition I could construct.\n- **This is a tolerance-derivation artifact, not a property of the math.** If the intent was to permit alternative implementations, the bound needs re-deriving against a reference perturbation rather than run-to-run variance of a deterministic kernel — the float64 row is the cleanest evidence. That's a finding for the harness (`artifacts/05` workload generation), and worth flagging before other L1 GEMM-shaped problems are scored the same way, since they'd inherit the same issue.\n\nI used 2 of 5 attempts; the exploration was done in throwaway local scripts (since removed) rather than against the verifier.","ttft_ms":3382,"ttft_stream_ms":3381,"time_to_request_ms":96,"type":"result","duration_ms":1038237,"uuid":"33ba95b2-8c36-4daf-8060-74073140412d"}
